Hi group,

My apologies, I accidentally deleted my last post on this topic. I have a quick question for how to figure out the number for K ("The maximum possible value of M, the super-population size") in gpcount. According to Chandler et al. 2011, "We again note that Mi is the total number of individuals that could possibly be detected at plot i over the duration of sampling." However, how is this number calculated? Here is an example of my data

     Visit1         Visit2         Visit3         Visit4         Visit5         Visit6         Visit7         Visit8         Visit9       Visit10        Visit11       Visit12       Visit13       Visit14       Visit15
3 3 3 1 2 0 2 2 2 0 1 3 3 1 2
7 1 1 2 1 1 0 1 1 0 2 0 3 2 1
3 1 1 0 4 1 2 1 1 3 3 2 3 3 0
6 1 0 0 0 0 4 0 1 1 0 3 5 1 1
2 2 1 2 1 0 1 1 1 2 2 2 5 0 3
4 3 1 0 1 1 2 4 0 1 1 1 2 2 1
2 3 2 3 2 2 2 2 2 2 1 2 3 2 0
4 2 1 1 0 1 1 0 1 0 0 2 1 0 6

Should I add across visits, take the average, or make an educated guess about all the individuals I may have missed?

Thanks for any advice!

Best,
Kate

